** Memory Usage

	A reasonable question to ask is how much RAM gets used to store objects in the packages. In general, the answer is <<more than you
	probably like>> if your design requirements include GPU speeds or tiny memory devices. If so, though, you probably aren't reading
	this because Java is not among your implementation options. I could be wrong, but Java's runtime engines (jre's) put enough distance
	between developers and any hardware that supports embarrassingly parallel problems to discourage jre uses.

	Having said that, cladosG objects are not huge until one tries to express algebras with many more generators than typically are
	considered in research problems. I've listed them in the table below with each row representing how many generators are used during
	object construction. For example, an algebra with 4 generators uses the first four generators from the Generator enumeration class.
	
	The current limit Clados supports is 15 generators. No doubt future devices will have much more RAM than current devices, but the
	way Monad is implemented has to change a bit at 16 generators because the internal grade key is a long integer. The current way
	the key is calculated overflows if 16 generators are used. No one is clamoring for 16 generator algebas as far as I know... or 
	wants one to occupy about 16GB of RAM before they even start creating multivectors using them. That means this limit is likely 
	to remain in place for a number of years.

